task: bench-cross (the last item of the operator's "take everything")
agent: okay-42
started: 2026-09-07
branch: feature/bench-cross

why: 55 modules cross-built for JVM, JS and Native, and every one of
  the 34 JMH lanes runs on the JVM only. The channel is a ring of
  CASes; on Native it runs on real threads with no Loom, on JS in one
  thread through the event loop. The library's performance story is
  known on a third of its platforms.

what: BenchCross, a munit suite in src/test/scala-cross that runs the
  same four shapes on whichever platform compiles it -- a Source.range
  fold, an elementwise channel read, a drainedChunks read, a Chunks
  fold -- N=4000, a warmup, then the median of 20 runs, printed as
  `bench-cross | <platform> | <lane> | <us/op>` lines. Tagged Live, so
  it is outside the default gate (a measurement is not a law), and
  run on purpose: `okayJVM/testOnly okay.BenchCross -- --include-tags=Live`,
  same for okayJS and okayNative. Platform from java.vm.name, no new
  source directory. Async-style (runAsync + Future) so one source
  serves all three; no CanBlock anywhere.

what is recorded: three columns per lane in a new section of
  docs/benchmarks.md, ledger rows, and whatever the ratios say -- the
  claim is the harness and the first numbers, not a conclusion. JMH
  on the JVM stays the reference; this is a ruler, not a scale.
